+# Pick
+
+**Winner: `candidates/b`**
+
+All three finalists pass the filter (40/40, `.grooph/line-diff/runs/20260920-190434/filter-output.txt:45-48`)
+and all three implement the same Myers O((N+M)·D) forward-trace / backtrack
+algorithm (`a/diff.mjs:34-103`, `b/diff.mjs:27-105`, `c/diff.mjs:19-85`). The
+choice therefore comes down to the three stated criteria.
+
+## Reads most clearly at its size
+
+- **b (122 lines)**: the two non-obvious pieces of Myers are each explained at
+  the point they matter: why `offset = max + 1` (`b/diff.mjs:31-35`) and which
+  direction each branch of the frontier step moves (`b/diff.mjs:49`, `:51`).
+  The header comment explains the algorithm against the LCS table it replaces
+  (`b/diff.mjs:3-9`). One wart: the `max === 0` guard at `b/diff.mjs:39-42` is
+  dead code, because `diffLines` already returns `[]` for two empty inputs at
+  `b/diff.mjs:117` before calling `shortestEditTrace`.
+- **a (120 lines)**: equally clean structure and a good header comment
+  (`a/diff.mjs:1-17`), but the frontier step (`a/diff.mjs:46-50`) is
+  uncommented, and `offset = max` (`a/diff.mjs:38`) is only in-bounds because
+  the empty-side cases are peeled off first at `a/diff.mjs:113-115`; that
+  dependency is not stated.
+- **c (108 lines)**: shortest, but the hardest to follow. The frontier is a
+  `Map` copied every step (`c/diff.mjs:23`, `:28`), `?? 0` is applied
+  inconsistently (present at `:32`, `:34`, `:62`, `:67`; absent at `:31`, which
+  only works because `undefined < 0` is `false`), `trace[d] = v` at `:43` is a
+  no-op assignment with a comment that suggests otherwise, and the backtrack is
+  a generator yielding index tuples with `-1` sentinels (`:71`, `:77`, `:79`)
+  that a second pass then maps back to lines (`:102-106`). That indirection
+  buys nothing over emitting `{ op, line }` directly as a and b do.
+
+## Stays fast on inputs of a few thousand lines
+
+- **b**: one `Int32Array` snapshot per edit distance (`b/diff.mjs:36`, `:45`),
+  so per-step cost is a typed-array memcpy. APPROACH reports measured figures:
+  about 1 ms for a 4,000-line input with a handful of edits and about 200 ms
+  for two unrelated 4,000-line inputs (`b/APPROACH.md:9-12`).
+- **a**: same shape but with a plain `Array` snapshot per step
+  (`a/diff.mjs:39`, `:43`), so the worst case holds the same number of slots at
+  a larger per-element size. No measurement is given (`a/APPROACH.md:12-19`
+  estimates only).
+- **c**: `new Map(v)` per step (`c/diff.mjs:28`) gives O(D²) frontier memory
+  but slow constants; c's own APPROACH reports roughly 2.7 seconds for two
+  disjoint 3,000-line inputs (`c/APPROACH.md:13-15`). That is inside "a few
+  thousand lines" and is more than ten times b's measured worst case. The
+  filter's small "long unrelated inputs" case points the same way: c 1.31 ms
+  (`filter-output.txt:41`) versus a 0.66 ms (`:15`) and b 0.79 ms (`:28`).
+
+## APPROACH.md explained honestly
+
+- **b**: states the algorithm, gives measured numbers for both the easy and
+  the worst-case shape, and admits the cost of the choice: "at the price of
+  being a less obvious algorithm to read and verify" (`b/APPROACH.md:14-15`).
+  Omission: it does not mention that the trace holds an (N+M)-slot snapshot per
+  edit distance, i.e. O((N+M)·D) memory.
+- **a**: honest about the O((N+M)²) worst case and about not special-casing it
+  (`a/APPROACH.md:16-22`), but the performance claim is an unmeasured estimate
+  ("on the order of tens of millions of array operations", `a/APPROACH.md:17-18`)
+  that counts only the inner loop and not the per-step `v.slice()` copy.
+- **c**: honest, including the unflattering 2.7 s worst case
+  (`c/APPROACH.md:13-15`) and a correct statement of its D² memory
+  (`c/APPROACH.md:10-11`). The honesty is a credit; the number it reports is
+  what disqualifies it on the speed criterion.
+
+## A smaller change beats a more general one
+
+None of the three is more general than the others: same contract, same
+algorithm, no extra options or exports. Sizes are within 14 lines of each
+other. This criterion does not separate a from b; it counts slightly against
+c only in the sense that its extra machinery (generator, sentinel remap, Map
+frontier) is more mechanism for the same result, not less.
+
+## Verdict
+
+`candidates/b`: clearest at the two points where Myers is genuinely tricky,
+the fastest worst case at the few-thousand-line scale with numbers to back
+it, and the most candid APPROACH. `candidates/a` is a close second and would
+be an acceptable pick; `candidates/c` is ruled out by its own worst-case
+measurement and by the readability cost of its indirection.
+
+## One idea worth borrowing (from `candidates/a`)
+
+State the memory cost, not just the time cost. a's header says the algorithm
+is "O((N+M)D) time and space" (`a/diff.mjs:11`) and its APPROACH repeats the
+tradeoff (`a/APPROACH.md:12-22`). b keeps one full `Int32Array` snapshot per
+edit distance (`b/diff.mjs:45`) and never says so in code or in
+`b/APPROACH.md`. For two unrelated inputs of a few thousand lines that is
+hundreds of megabytes; a one-line note in b's header comment and APPROACH
+would make its performance story complete.
+
+Secondary, not a borrow but a trim the lead may want to route: the dead
+`max === 0` branch at `b/diff.mjs:39-42` can be removed without changing
+behaviour, since `b/diff.mjs:117` already handles that case.
