+# Approach
+
+`diffLines` implements the Myers O(ND) shortest-edit-script algorithm directly
+(no library), where D is the number of `-`/`+` entries in the final script:
+it walks a forward "frontier" of furthest-reaching diagonals for each edit
+distance `d`, recording each frontier, then backtracks from `(n, m)` to
+`(0, 0)` through the recorded frontiers to recover the actual sequence of
+matches, deletions and insertions, which is reversed into the final script.
+Inputs are validated up front (`TypeError` for anything that isn't an
+array of strings) and copied with `.slice()` before use so the originals are
+never mutated; empty-input cases are special-cased for clarity and speed.
+Performance is input-shape dependent: because cost is O((N+M)\*D), diffs of a
+few thousand mostly-similar lines (the realistic case for a review tool,
+e.g. a few edits scattered through 3-5k lines) run in well under a second
+since D stays small (tens to hundreds of edits), whereas two totally
+unrelated inputs of a few thousand lines each (D close to N+M) push the
+algorithm toward its O((N+M)^2) worst case -- still on the order of tens of
+millions of array operations, which is a noticeable but tolerable fraction
+of a second in Node, not a hang. No attempt was made to special-case that
+worst case (e.g. via Hirschberg space-halving or a heuristic diagonal cutoff)
+since the contract only asks for correctness and minimality, not a bound on
+pathological inputs.
